new file mode 100644
index 0000000000..06cedef856
--- /dev/null
+++ b/packages/fcl-tls/README.md
@@ -0,0 +1,180 @@
+# Client TLS selection and native Windows TLS
+
+This increment adds a Windows Schannel client and an opt-in `smartsslsockets`
+unit. It targets this FPC 3.3.1 source branch. macOS native TLS remains a design
+in [docs/macos-transport.md](docs/macos-transport.md); it is not implemented.
+
+```pascal
+uses fphttpclient, smartsslsockets;
+var Client: TFPHTTPClient;
+begin
+  Client:=TFPHTTPClient.Create(nil);
+  try
+    Client.VerifySSLCertificate:=True;
+    Writeln(Client.Get('https://example.com/'));
+  finally Client.Free end;
+end.
+```
+
+On Windows, with no supported TLS DLL alongside the executable, this uses
+Schannel and, with verification enabled, Windows certificate trust without a third party TLS DLL.
+`schannelsslsockets` can also be used directly in place of `smartsslsockets`.
+
+## Selection contract
+
+Selection happens on the first `TSSLSocketHandler.GetDefaultHandlerClass` or
+`GetDefaultHandler` call. The selected class and any failure are cached under a
+critical section; later calls do not retry loading or change providers.
+
+1. A supported application-directory OpenSSL pair takes priority.
+2. If no pair member exists, application-directory GnuTLS is considered.
+3. If neither exists, Windows selects Schannel. Other platforms report that no
+   native adapter is available in this increment.
+
+Detection uses the executable's absolute directory, independently of the working
+directory and PATH. OpenSSL names follow `openssl.SSL_DLL_Names` and their matched
+crypto names on Windows; GnuTLS names are `libgnutls-30.dll`, then `libgnutls.dll`.
+Unix candidates are OpenSSL 3, 1.1, then unversioned library pairs, and GnuTLS
+30, then unversioned. Architecture and dependencies are checked by the platform
+loader; Windows also checks the PE machine before loading. Required client and
+verification exports must exist. An incomplete, corrupt, wrong-architecture or
+incompatible library is an error, not permission to move to a lower-priority
+backend. On Windows, safe DLL dependency-search flags must be supported by the OS.
+
+Configure overrides before the first factory call, including failed calls:
+
+```pascal
+SetSmartTLSBackend(tbSchannel); // bypass bundled libraries explicitly
+// Or:
+SetSmartTLSBackend(tbOpenSSL);
+SetSmartTLSLibraryPaths('C:\MyApp\libssl-3-x64.dll',
+  'C:\MyApp\libcrypto-3-x64.dll', '');
+```
+
+`GetSmartTLSBackend`, `TLSBackendName` and `GetSmartTLSLibraryPath` expose the
+selection without triggering it. Until successful selection, the backend is
+`tbAuto`. Configuration becomes immutable when selection starts. Explicit paths
+must be absolute and the OpenSSL pair must be supplied together. Missing
+explicit paths are errors. No environment variable implicitly changes policy.
+
+The existing explicit `opensslsockets` / `gnutlssockets` registration and usage
+remain available. `SetDefaultHandlerClass` clears the optional new lazy factory.
+The smart unit depends on its backends, so its initialization runs after theirs;
+its priority does not depend on the application's `uses` order.
+
+Smart selection owns TLS library initialization. Previously initialized bindings
+for a selected library are rejected. The new binding-level acquire APIs compare
+the pinned module handles under the same lock as legacy initialization. A
+competing initializer cannot silently substitute another module. Successful
+selection claims the binding until unit finalization: `DestroySSLInterface`
+returns false while claimed; `FreeGnuTLS` and loading a different GnuTLS path
+raise an exception. Existing explicit usage without a claim retains its behavior.
+The application must not release another owner's claim or directly overwrite
+global binding pointers.
+
+## Validation and client scope
+
+`VerifyPeerCert` and `fphttpclient.VerifySSLCertificate` keep the target
+branch's existing default, False. Set True explicitly for chain and hostname
+validation. False disables both checks for that connection; it does not change
+TLS protocol selection, encryption, backend selection or system security settings.
+`AfterSocketHandlerCreate` can override the handler option before connection.
+A custom `OnGetSocketHandler` result keeps the existing caller-owned setup rules.
+
+With True, Schannel uses automatic Windows trust/hostname validation. Smart
+OpenSSL installs its configured default CA paths, uses DNS or IP SAN matching as
+appropriate, and can also load the client's CA file/directory. Applications may
+need to supply OpenSSL CA material on Windows. GnuTLS uses its configured/system
+trust and enables hostname-aware handshake verification only with True. The
+smart GnuTLS verification-status check is likewise conditional. No provider
+fallback or implicit verification downgrade happens after TLS begins.
+
+The application callback retains its existing post-handshake semantics: it can
+reject an otherwise successful connection, but cannot rescue a handshake that
+already failed certificate verification. OpenSSL and Schannel invoke it after a
+successful handshake for either switch value; GnuTLS preserves its existing
+True-only callback behavior. This increment does not redefine it as a callback
+for individual validation errors.
+Client-only `Accept` fails explicitly. No application cryptography is implemented.
+
+Schannel supports the default OS protocol policy (`stAny`) and explicit TLS 1.2.
+It probes modern `SCH_CREDENTIALS` before using legacy credentials on systems
+that report those credentials unsupported, before any TLS handshake. This does
+not change FPC's OS compatibility baseline. Supported TLS versions depend on the
+OS policy; current tests cover Windows 10 x64 and TLS 1.2, not older Windows,
+Windows 11 TLS 1.3, 32-bit or ARM64.
+
+The first native client increment explicitly rejects client certificates,
+application CA files/directories, custom ciphers, ALPN and disabling SNI. These
+capabilities are not silently ignored. `CertificateData.HostName` remains the
+certificate-generation field; the optional Schannel `ServerName` property is
+the authenticated peer name when TCP connects to an IP or a tunnel. Otherwise
+the `TInetSocket` peer hostname is used. Callers should pass ASCII/IDNA hostnames.
+
+Schannel preserves incomplete TLS records, unconsumed encrypted bytes and
+decrypted leftovers. It handles `SEC_I_RENEGOTIATE` by returning to ISC with the
+same trust policy and preserves plaintext returned with renegotiation or
+`close_notify`. A TCP EOF without `close_notify` is a truncation error. Handshake
+uses `ConnectTimeout`, falling back to `IOTimeout`; send/receive operations use
+`IOTimeout`. The handler owns a nonblocking TCP transport and waits with the
+same socket deadline on partial sends/receives, including backpressure. Windows
+SSPI credential and trust calls are synchronous; socket deadlines do not cancel
+work inside those OS APIs. Closing has a bounded wait even if no I/O timeout was set. A handler
+has one owner: simultaneous operations on the same handler are not supported.
+
+## Build and reproduce
+
+Use the existing bootstrap compiler and GNU make from the FPC bin directory.
+FPC 3.2.2 cannot directly build the current `TNullPtr`-using library sources;
+build this branch's compiler and RTL first. Example from the source root:
+
+```powershell
+$env:PATH = 'C:\lazarus\fpc\3.2.2\bin\x86_64-win64;' + $env:PATH
+make -C compiler cycle FPC=C:/lazarus/fpc/3.2.2/bin/x86_64-win64/ppcx64.exe -j4
+make -C packages all FPC=D:/Projects/fpc/source/compiler/ppcx64.exe 'FPMAKEOPT=-T 4 -sap' -j4
+```
+
+The package build compiles unit targets for the current Windows target and skips
+example programs. It does not install anything. The test runner builds into the
+chosen output directory and preserves `results.log`:
+
+```powershell
+& .\packages\fcl-tls\tests\run.ps1 `
+  -OutputDirectory "$env:TEMP\fpc-native-tls-tests" `
+  -OpenSSLLibrary 'C:\Program Files\Git\mingw64\bin\libssl-3-x64.dll' `
+  -CryptoLibrary 'C:\Program Files\Git\mingw64\bin\libcrypto-3-x64.dll' `
+  -OpenSSLExecutable 'C:\Program Files\Git\mingw64\bin\openssl.exe' `
+  -OpenSSLCAFile 'C:\Program Files\Git\usr\ssl\certs\ca-bundle.crt' -Online
+```
+
+Omit `-Online` for tests without public-network access. Supply only already
+installed library fixtures; nothing is downloaded. The loader matrix creates
+isolated application directories. Supplying `-OpenSSLExecutable` adds 28 real
+local TLS tests even without `-Online`: temporary CA/leaf certificates, TLS
+1.2/1.3, DNS/IPv4/IPv6 identities, rejected chains and an intentionally truncated
+handshake. A Python SSL server binds loopback; its CA is supplied only in each
+child client's environment, not installed into any trust store. Test keys are
+disposable fixtures, not application credentials. The optional public runner
+temporarily sets the current PowerShell process's CA environment and restores it.
+
+`teststates.py` uses a test-only SSPI boundary
+provider, separately compiled, to test rare state transitions and send/close backpressure; it does not prove
+cryptography or real OS trust behavior. Public tests use real Schannel and retain
+system validation. The relay forwards ciphertext and splits TCP fragments; it
+does not decrypt traffic or change trust. A certificate test requires the expected
+Schannel validation code; connection errors/timeouts fail the test.
+
+Public EOF diagnosis and its bounded reproduction command are in
+[docs/openssl-eof.md](docs/openssl-eof.md). A diagnostic `probe` process may exit
+zero after collecting a failed handshake; its output never counts as a passing
+certificate test. No diagnostic configuration changes production selection or
+adds a retry/downgrade.
+
+See [docs/validation.md](docs/validation.md) for the observed results and gaps.
+
+## API references
+
+- [Microsoft Schannel credential policy](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/api/schannel/ns-schannel-schannel_cred)
+- [Microsoft Schannel decryption and renegotiation](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/secauthn/decryptmessage--schannel)
+- [Microsoft Schannel shutdown](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/secauthn/shutting-down-an-schannel-connection)
+- [GnuTLS session verification](https://gnutls.org/manual/html_node/Core-TLS-API.html#gnutls_005fsession_005fset_005fverify_005fcert)
diff --git a/packages/fcl-tls/docs/macos-transport.md b/packages/fcl-tls/docs/macos-transport.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000000..d879e3b53f
--- /dev/null
+++ b/packages/fcl-tls/docs/macos-transport.md
@@ -0,0 +1,192 @@
+# Proposed macOS connection adapter
+
+This is a design for the next increment, not implemented or tested macOS code.
+
+## Existing ownership constraint
+
+`TInetSocket` currently creates a raw TCP socket, completes the TCP connection,
+then invokes `TSocketHandler.Connect`. Schannel and the existing third party
+backends wrap that socket. Network.framework creates and owns its TCP+TLS
+connection. Registering another `TSSLSocketHandler` after TCP connect would make
+a second connection, leave socket readiness/endpoints inconsistent and bypass
+existing timeout/close semantics. Do not implement it through a fake file
+descriptor or a second hidden TCP connection.
+
+## Recommended seam
+
+Introduce a client connection stream abstraction in fcl-net, with no dependencies
+on TLS providers. Keep `TInetSocket` and its socket handlers unchanged for existing
+users; a socket-backed adapter wraps them for the new HTTP client seam. Add an
+optional connection factory used before any TCP socket is created. A proposed
+interface for the next implementation stage:
+
+```pascal
+TClientConnection = class(TStream)
+  procedure Connect(const Host: String; Port: Word); virtual; abstract;
+  function CanRead(Timeout: Integer): Boolean; virtual; abstract;
+  procedure Close; virtual; abstract;
+  // Contract also includes ConnectTimeout, IOTimeout, PeerClosed,
+  // error descriptions, authenticated peer, negotiated ALPN and endpoints.
+end;
+TClientConnectionFactory = function: TClientConnection;
+```
+
+`fphttpclient` would get a virtual `CreateClientConnection` hook and retain its
+existing socket path as the default. Review each current `TSocketStream` use
+(timeouts, proxy handling, EOF, keep-alive, extract socket, endpoint access) before
+changing its field type. Preserve public socket APIs by exposing a socket only
+for socket-backed connections; document a native transport's absence explicitly.
+The smart selector owns both handler and transport factory selection under the
+same lock. Direct legacy `TSSLSocketHandler.GetDefaultHandler` callers cannot
+use a Network.framework-only adapter and must receive a clear capability error.
+
+An alternative is to make `TSocketStream` support a transport without a socket
+and add an ownership capability to `TSocketHandler`. That touches constructors,
+raw close, endpoint getters and readiness extensively; the dedicated client
+connection seam makes ownership easier to review. A preconnected-socket bridge
+is not recommended for Network.framework.
+
+## Network.framework adapter
+
+Create one `nw_connection` from the logical hostname, port and TCP+TLS parameters.
+Use a dedicated serial dispatch queue for state/send/receive callbacks. Adapt
+synchronous `TStream` operations with bounded queues and condition events;
+never block the callback queue while waiting for a completion on that queue.
+Retain callback blocks, the connection and data until each completion finishes.
+Cancel on deadlines, wake waiters exactly once and make repeated close safe.
+Prevent late callbacks from accessing a freed Pascal stream.
+
+Honor the existing False default and explicit VerifyPeerCert setting. With
+True, keep platform system trust and hostname verification enabled. With False,
+a per-connection verification block may accept the supplied peer without those
+checks; do not change TLS protocol policy, OS settings or the selected backend.
+Keep application callbacks as post-handshake vetoes, rather than changing them
+into callbacks for individual certificate errors. Treat verify, readiness and send failures
+as terminal for the selected provider. Enable ALPN through TLS protocol options
+when implemented and return the platform's selected protocol. No software crypto.
+
+Runtime-detect the system framework and required symbols. Do not raise the FPC
+branch's baseline to Network.framework's availability. Older macOS without the
+framework reports no native backend after absent bundled libraries. Secure
+Transport support remains an explicit future decision, not an implicit fallback.
+Preserve modern macOS certificate trust and sandbox behavior. Proxy support and
+client credentials need explicit capability decisions before an implementation
+claims parity with the socket path.
+
+## Required macOS acceptance
+
+Build and run on actual x86_64 and arm64 macOS, covering the target branch's
+compiler baseline and supported framework versions. Test absent/broken bundled
+libraries, default system trust, wrong hostname/expired/untrusted certificates,
+fragmented multi-megabyte reads/writes, timeouts during connect and I/O,
+cancel-vs-completion races, clean EOF, repeated close, keep-alive, ALPN and proxy
+capabilities. Add checks for callback lifetime and no second TCP connection.
+Windows results and simulated SSPI tests provide no macOS runtime evidence.
+
+The owner subsequently created and authorized ACTom/fpc-macos-ci for the
+necessary test payload. It is public and was empty on inspection. The first
+manual preflight is prepared below; no run has been started.
+
+## Observed Windows host toolchain
+
+Read-only checks on 2026-10-05 found the following. They cover PATH and known
+FPC/RAD Studio installation and SDK-cache locations, not every directory on disk.
+
+- The built FPC 3.3.1 x86_64 compiler lists Darwin as a target and accepts
+  `-Tdarwin`. Its native code-generation backend also recognizes CBLOCKS.
+- Installed and built RTL directories contain only `x86_64-win64`. A minimal
+  `-Tdarwin -Cn` program actually failed with `Can't find unit system`; no
+  executable was linked. This is a missing target RTL, not a Network.framework
+  compilation or runtime result.
+- No `ppca64` / `ppcrossx64`, clang, lld, xcrun or xcodebuild was found on PATH.
+- Existing RAD Studio 37 contains Delphi `dccosx64` / `dccosxarm64`, which do not
+  supply FPC units. Its `bin/ld.exe -v` reports an LD64 Windows port (951_9),
+  with x86_64/arm64 support and LLVM 15.0.7 LTO support. Thus a Mach-O linker
+  exists on this machine even though it is not on PATH. Compatibility with FPC
+  link command/options has not been established.
+- No Apple SDK was found in the repository, known Lazarus/FPC target directories
+  or the checked personal/public RAD Studio SDK caches. Existing univint sources
+  do not declare `nw_connection_create`, `sec_protocol_options_set_verify_block`
+  or `dispatch_queue_create` in the checked source subtree.
+
+## Conditions for the next implementation stage
+
+1. Confirm the client connection factory seam before changing HTTP stream
+   ownership. Implement and test the existing socket adapter on Windows first.
+2. Supply/build compatible x86_64 Darwin RTL/package units and an aarch64 FPC
+   cross compiler plus target RTL. Compiler source target support alone is not
+   a usable target runtime or a successful cross build.
+3. Provide an Apple-authorized SDK/framework interface set through the owner's
+   existing development environment, and verify the selected Mach-O linker with
+   FPC. Network.framework, Security, libSystem/dispatch and callback-block ABI
+   bindings need target compilation and lifetime checks. No SDK has been
+   downloaded, copied from an unrelated project or substituted during this work.
+4. Provide real macOS x86_64 and arm64 execution for network/trust, callbacks,
+   timeout/cancellation and connection ownership. Windows cross compilation
+   cannot establish that these platform behaviors work.
+5. Decide how older macOS without required Network.framework APIs behaves,
+   including whether a Secure Transport adapter is in scope. Keep the FPC branch
+   baseline unchanged and use runtime capability detection.
+
+The prepared CI uses only the runner's existing Xcode SDK and tools. No SDK
+or software is installed on the Windows host. FPC bootstrap provisioning is a
+separate unresolved requirement; this first preflight does not download one.
+The installed linker permits further toolchain investigation; it does not remove
+the missing SDK/RTL and real-runtime blockers.
+
+## References
+
+- [Apple Network.framework](https://developer.apple.com/documentation/network)
+- [Apple nw_connection_create](https://developer.apple.com/documentation/network/nw_connection_create(_:_:))
+- [Apple TLS verification callback](https://developer.apple.com/documentation/security/sec_protocol_options_set_verify_block(_:_:_:))
+
+## Current HTTP seam audit and bounded implementation plan
+
+The HTTP client owns FSocket as TSocketStream (fphttpclient.pp:151); normal
+connection creation is at :845-850, reads/writes at :893/:898, IOTimeout updates
+at :693-694, and IsConnected currently means FSocket is assigned (:705).
+Socket/ExtractSocket expose that concrete type (:387/:802). Server-sent events
+also take ownership of it (:1614) and access its Handler.BytesAvailable (:2757).
+These are the concrete reasons a Network.framework handler cannot be bolted on
+after TInetSocket's existing TCP connect.
+
+The next production change is a pre-TCP client-connection factory plus one stream
+owner: a socket-backed adapter retains existing handler setup/event order; a
+Network.framework adapter owns one nw_connection. HTTP reads/writes, readiness,
+timeouts, connected state and close move through that connection abstraction.
+The old Socket/ExtractSocket paths retain ownership semantics for socket-backed
+connections. Native socket extraction must report an explicit capability error;
+an ExtractConnection API is needed for native ownership transfer. The first
+native HTTP scope must reject unsupported proxy/Unix-socket/SSE extraction,
+custom client credentials and ALPN rather than silently ignoring them. Each
+capability can be added with its own runtime test after the basic adapter works.
+
+Implement/test the neutral socket adapter and field ownership on Windows before
+replacing any HTTP path. Then compile Darwin bindings (or a small C ABI shim)
+against the installed Apple SDK, bridge serial-queue completions to bounded
+synchronous stream operations, and test cancelled/late callback lifetime on real
+macOS. This is a public ownership-interface change; it is not hidden in this
+preflight, and no HTTP ownership change has yet been made.
+
+## Prepared first macOS CI stage
+
+`tests/macos/networkframework-api.c` is a construction probe. It compiles the
+actual Apple SDK's Network/Security/dispatch types and callback blocks, constructs
+both verification configurations, and releases them. It never calls
+nw_connection_start, opens no TCP connection, and supplies no HTTPS, certificate,
+queue cancellation or Pascal-runtime evidence. It is not a production backend.
+All CI orchestration is maintained separately in
+[ACTom/fpc-macos-ci](https://github.com/ACTom/fpc-macos-ci). Its `ci/preflight.sh`
+checks the actual architecture and existing SDK/tools; absence of an existing
+FPC is explicitly reported as NOT RUN, not installed. No GitHub Actions workflow
+or CI-only runner script is included in this FPC source patch.
+
+The test repository's `.github/workflows/macos-preflight.yml` configures only
+workflow_dispatch: no push, pull_request or schedule triggers. Exactly two standard jobs use macos-15-intel (x86_64) and
+macos-15 (arm64), each bounded to 15 minutes, with concurrency limiting duplicate
+runs. They verify a SHA256-fixed patch against exact base 56900cd3a58b32cf3c0f8a3b48443a139bab8e0d,
+download one source snapshot without mirroring history, then compile/link/run the
+API probe using the runner's existing authorized Apple SDK. First-run approval
+is still required. This stage does not claim FPC Darwin compilation or native TLS
+acceptance. A usable Darwin bootstrap compiler/RTL plus the adapter and real TLS
+matrix are subsequent work, subject to explicit tool provisioning when needed.
