+# Windows increment validation
+
+Recorded on 2026-10-05 against base commit
+`56900cd3a5` (`Update FileDialog demo.`), branch
+`feature/native-tls-smart-sockets`. The repository was clean before this work.
+No reset, commit, push, remote publication or software installation was performed.
+
+## Environment and build
+
+- Windows 10 x64, OS build 19044.
+- Existing Lazarus FPC 3.2.2 x86_64-win64 bootstrap and GNU make 3.80.
+- This source branch's FPC 3.3.1 compiler and RTL were built with `compiler cycle`.
+- `packages all` with `FPMAKEOPT=-T 4 -sap` built Windows package unit targets,
+  including fcl-net, OpenSSL, GnuTLS and the new fcl-tls package. Examples are
+  excluded by that option. This is not the compiler's complete regression suite.
+- Existing Git OpenSSL 3.5.4 DLLs and Git's CA bundle were used as fixtures.
+  The test process temporarily sets `SSL_CERT_FILE`; it restores the prior value
+  and does not modify the Windows certificate store.
+- Build/test outputs are outside the source tree in the task's `tls-build`
+  directory. `packages-final.log` and `final-results/results.log` are the final
+  build and test logs; earlier runs remain in separately named directories.
+
+Use [../tests/run.ps1](../tests/run.ps1) and the commands in
+[../README.md](../README.md) to reproduce. Without `-Online`, only deterministic
+local tests and supplied-library loader tests run. No software is downloaded.
+
+## Initial increment checks (before the switch compatibility correction)
+
+| Area | Evidence |
+| --- | --- |
+| Pure selection policy | 13 checks: OpenSSL before GnuTLS before native; explicit overrides; invalid/absent libraries; unavailable native backend |
+| Lazy factory compatibility | Deferred factory call; explicit class registration clears the factory; nil native certificate generator is safe; unsupported certificate generation fails |
+| Concurrent smart construction | 16 threads, 100 constructions per thread, for both native and checked OpenSSL selection |
+| Actual Windows loader | 17 isolated application-directory cases, including broken/incomplete pairs, missing exports, wrong architecture, ignored working-directory DLLs, overrides and preinitialized bindings |
+| External initializer competition | 50 competing legacy OpenSSL initializations; pinned module identity remains consistent; mismatched acquisition fails; claimed binding cannot be destroyed |
+| GnuTLS loader ownership | Harmless stub module verifies that a claimed binding cannot be freed, reloaded or acquired with a different handle; this is not a real GnuTLS handshake |
+| No third party runtime | Absent-library native factory checks that neither global OpenSSL nor GnuTLS binding was initialized |
+| Client-only boundary | Schannel Accept fails with an error and never activates TLS; smart third party wrappers explicitly reject Accept |
+| Real HTTP composition | fphttpclient exercises the smart factory with explicit Schannel, default verification switch left false |
+| Real Windows trust | example.com succeeds; badssl wrong hostname, expired, self-signed and untrusted-root certificates produce strict native validation errors |
+| Fragmented receive | 1 MiB response across about 4,100 ciphertext relay writes; one-byte header reads and decrypted leftovers; also forced TLS 1.2 |
+| Large send | 1 MiB TLS upload and authenticated HTTP response through the ciphertext relay |
+| Socket deadlines | Stalled handshake and receive timeout with 150 ms configuration |
+| Shutdown | Real close_notify generation, deactivated TLS state and repeated shutdown |
+| Rare SSPI transitions | Isolated scripted boundary provider tests incomplete encrypted records, buffered plaintext, renegotiation without EXTRA, plaintext with close_notify and clean TLS EOF |
+| Send/close backpressure | Same production handler against loopback with small buffers and a scripted SSPI provider; send expires around 150 ms and default no-IOTimeout close around 2 seconds |
+| Smart OpenSSL trust | Actual OpenSSL 3.5.4 trusted DNS and IPv4 SAN endpoints, plus certificate rejection checks, while fphttpclient's legacy verification switch is false |
+
+Native certificate errors observed:
+
+- Wrong hostname: `SEC_E_WRONG_PRINCIPAL`, `0x80090322`.
+- Expired certificate: `SEC_E_CERT_EXPIRED`, `0x80090328`.
+- Self-signed and untrusted root: `SEC_E_UNTRUSTED_ROOT`, `0x80090325`.
+
+The negative tests require a certificate validation error. Socket timeout,
+unexpected EOF or another network failure makes the test fail, rather than being
+counted as a successful rejection. Selection stays frozen after a handshake
+failure; those tests also assert the selected backend remains unchanged.
+
+## Review and observed failures
+
+An independent read-only review found three important issues, fixed in this
+increment and reviewed again: a probe/legacy loader race, blocking sends exceeding
+socket deadlines, and OpenSSL 1.1.1 IP literals using DNS verification rules.
+The final read-only review found no residual critical/important issue within
+those three fixes. The reviewer did not independently run the tests.
+
+The binding acquire APIs now compare pinned module identities atomically and
+claim them until smart unit finalization. Schannel uses nonblocking TCP with
+WOULDBLOCK retries under one deadline. A small protected OpenSSL peer-identity
+hook prevents legacy Connect from overwriting the smart IP rule; explicit legacy
+clients keep their previous behavior.
+
+Earlier runner attempts exposed a PowerShell Tee-Object parameter incompatibility
+and an OpenSSL IP endpoint returning HTTP 301 with an empty body. Both test-harness
+problems were corrected. The IP endpoint's authenticated 301 is now accepted
+without following it, so the test still verifies the original IP connection.
+
+Some earlier public endpoint tests failed with a TCP/TLS timeout or unexpected
+EOF. Their logs are retained, including a native wrong-host timeout and OpenSSL
+expired/self-signed/untrusted-root EOFs on different runs. None was counted as a
+certificate rejection. Public network conditions remain an external source of
+test variability; the final run outcome is recorded below.
+
+## Remaining acceptance gaps
+
+- Real GnuTLS TLS access and certificate matrix: no GnuTLS runtime is installed
+  in the available environment. Compilation, symbol guards and loader ownership
+  tests do not establish runtime handshake correctness.
+- OpenSSL 1.1.1 actual runtime: not available/tested here. OpenSSL 3 now has
+  local IPv4/IPv6 SAN evidence, which is not a substitute for version 1.1.1.
+- Real Schannel renegotiation, post-handshake authentication and TLS 1.3: the
+  rare control-flow cases use a simulated SSPI boundary provider. Windows 10
+  here does not provide the supported Schannel TLS 1.3 baseline; Windows 11 or
+  Server 2022 testing remains required.
+- Older supported Windows, Win32 and ARM64 builds/runs, dotted-unit build and
+  the complete compiler regression suite: not run.
+- Schannel custom trust, client certificates, ALPN and custom cipher configuration
+  are explicitly unsupported in this client increment, rather than ignored.
+- Windows SSPI credential and system trust calls are synchronous. Socket
+  deadlines bound network waits and partial sends; they cannot cancel work
+  inside the OS trust APIs. One handler has a single owner; simultaneous
+  operations on the same handler are unsupported.
+- macOS implementation and runtime validation: not started. The separate
+  [macOS transport design](macos-transport.md) describes the pre-TCP connection
+  factory required by Network.framework. Older macOS Secure Transport support
+  and the architecture choice require a later decision.
+- No public repository, MR or macOS CI job exists. Publication remains outside
+  the authorization for this increment.
+
+## Recorded build and public run outcome
+
+`packages-final.log`: exit 0. `git diff --check`: exit 0.
+
+`final-results/results.log`: the full optional-online runner exited 1 with one
+failure, smart OpenSSL against `untrusted-root.badssl.com`, which returned
+`unexpected eof while reading`. This run does not prove OpenSSL rejected that
+endpoint's certificate. A previous strict run did reach certificate verification
+and rejected it; the inconsistent public-network result remains recorded rather
+than hidden or counted as a pass.
+
+Every Schannel, policy, factory, loader, ownership, scripted-state and transport
+test in that final run passed. Schannel validated example.com (HTTP 200, 577
+bytes) and rejected all four invalid-certificate endpoints with the expected
+HRESULTs. The native 1 MiB receive, upload, forced TLS 1.2, handshake/read timeout
+and close checks passed. Scripted send pressure expired at 156 ms and default
+close pressure at 2031 ms.
+
+The same run's smart OpenSSL 3.5.4 checks passed trusted DNS, trusted IPv4 SAN
+(HTTP 301 from 1.1.1.1), wrong hostname, expired and self-signed certificate
+rejection, with the legacy verification switch false. No additional network
+retry was used to turn the failed full run into an all-pass claim.
+
+The final deterministic offline run (offline-final/results.log) exited 0,
+including the same 17 loader cases, binding race/claims, four scripted SSPI
+scenarios and local handshake deadline. No source implementation changed after
+the final build and online run.
+
+## Deterministic real TLS follow-up
+
+The same branch now includes `testlocaltls.py` and `testopensslpeer.pp`.
+`deterministic-final/results.log` exited 0, including all previous offline checks
+and 28 real TLS cases. The client uses Git OpenSSL 3.5.4; the Python server uses
+OpenSSL 3.0.19. Each case binds loopback and supplies only the trusted disposable
+root through its client subprocess environment. No system certificate store is
+changed and no software is installed.
+
+Under both fixed TLS 1.2 and TLS 1.3, tests passed trusted DNS/IPv4, wrong DNS (62),
+expired (10), self-signed (18), untrusted root without a supplied root (20),
+untrusted complete chain (19), and numeric DNS SAN that cannot authenticate an
+IP literal (64). Separate loopback IPv6 success and rejection passed. A default
+automatic-protocol client against a TLS1.2-only server succeeded for a trusted
+chain and rejected an untrusted complete chain. fphttpclient succeeded on the
+trusted case and rejected all five negative certificate cases with its legacy
+verification switch false.
+
+Deliberately closing TCP after ClientHello, before sending a certificate,
+reproduced `0A000126` EOF under both protocols. Those cases require handshake
+failure, inactive TLS and verification result 0 (the uninitialized/default
+verdict); they explicitly exclude certificate rejection. Raw server actions,
+negotiated versions, client stdout/stderr and exit codes are saved in
+`deterministic-final/local-tls/results.json`.
+
+Independent public OpenSSL command-line and FPC probes reproduced early EOF;
+see [the bounded diagnostic report](openssl-eof.md). The original online exit 1
+remains recorded above. The checked-in public diagnostic script also ran
+successfully as a collector; its explicit TLS 1.2 FPC probe returned EOF,
+so the collector exit 0 is not a validation pass or a protocol workaround.
+The local exit 0 supplies separate deterministic
+certificate evidence and does not convert that public failure into a pass.
+No TLS runtime source was changed during this diagnostic follow-up. A read-only
+review of the new assertions found no important issue; the reviewer did not
+independently execute the tests.
+
+## Verification switch compatibility correction
+
+The previous strict-smart policy has been replaced after the interface audit.
+Current smart and Schannel handlers honor VerifyPeerCert; HTTP clients keep the
+existing VerifySSLCertificate default False. True enables chain/hostname checks;
+False only disables those checks. No protocol fallback, provider switch or
+system-trust setting is changed. GnuTLS's previously unconditional handshake
+validation is now gated by the same option. Post-handshake callbacks are unchanged.
+
+`verify-red/results.json` records the test-first reproduction: the traditional
+OpenSSL control succeeded with False and a self-signed loopback server, while
+the previous smart/Schannel implementations incorrectly rejected it.
+
+`verify-expanded/results.log` exited 0, with 87 real local TLS cases. Those include
+the previous 28 strict checks (now explicitly True), False/default/after-create
+successes against wrong-DNS, expired, self-signed and untrusted chains, callback
+vetoes, six same-process/same-client False-to-True or True-to-False sequences,
+and ten additional False/OpenSSL TLS1.3 connections. The Python server observed
+encrypted TLS and HTTP requests; `verify-expanded/local-tls/results.json` retains
+the raw evidence. The sequences recreate handlers, rather than proving reuse of
+the exact same underlying handler. Schannel's local True negatives encounter the
+untrusted disposable root first; OpenSSL isolates DNS/expiry with its child-only
+CA file. No temporary root was added to Windows trust.
+
+The complete offline runner also passed policy, lazy factory, 17 loader cases,
+binding ownership/50 competing initializations, scripted SSPI state/deadline
+checks and handshake timeout. `packages-verify.log` records package build exit 0.
+A read-only independent review found no Critical/Important defect and verified
+the two-request assertions and recorded TLS1.3 evidence.
+
+`verify-expanded/native-online.log` exited 1: example.com, wrong hostname,
+self-signed/untrusted-root rejection, a True-mode application veto, 1 MiB upload
+and fragmented download, read timeout and explicit TLS1.2 transport passed.
+The expired.badssl.com attempt ended with TCP truncation before certificate
+verification and remains a failure, not a successful certificate rejection.
+The earlier public OpenSSL EOF failure remains recorded above as well.
+
+No real GnuTLS library, OpenSSL1.1.1, Win32/ARM64/older-Windows or Schannel TLS1.3
+runtime was tested. A separate existing GnuTLS same-handler reconnect/session
+ownership issue was confirmed unchanged in the base commit; HTTP tests here
+create fresh handlers. macOS execution remains pending the prepared CI stage.
